{"id":"pddl-tpp-planning","benchmark":"skillsbench","instruction":"Solve travelling purchase problem (TPP) tasks using PDDL (Planning Domain Definition Language).\n\nEach task has two input files: a PDDL domain file and a PDDL problem file. As a planning agent, you may need both of them. The domain and problem file paths for each task are specified the \"domain\" key and \"problem\" key in the `problem.json` file. An example task entry looks like below:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n  \"id\": \"problem_id\",\n  \"domain\": \".../xxx.pddl\",\n  \"problem\": \".../yyy.pddl\",\n  \"plan_output\": \"xxx/problem_id.txt\"\n  },\n  ...,\n]\n```\n\nFor each task specified in `problem.json`, you need to: First, load the PDDL domain file and PDDL problem file. Second, generate a PDDL plan for solving the planning problem. Finally, write the generated plan to the path specified by “plan_output”. An example PDDL plan looks like:\n\n```\ndrive(truck1, depot1, market1)\nbuy(truck1, goods1, market1, level0, level1, level0, level1)\nload(goods1, truck1, market1, level0, level1, level0, level1)\ndrive(truck1, market1, depot1)\nunload(goods1, truck1, depot1, level0, level1, level0, level1)\n```\n\nNote that\n\n- The plan should be a syntactically correct PDDL plan.\n- The plan should be valid, it should solve the problem when executed according to the PDDL grammar.\n- Each action primitive should be written on a line.\n- Action names and object names in the generated plan should match the PDDL domain and PDDL problem.","supplemental":[],"items":[{"item_id":"S-01","requirement":"For every task listed in problem.json, a plan output file must be written at the exact path given by that task's \"plan_output\" field.","source_quote":"write the generated plan to the path specified by “plan_output”","verification_type":"assertion","verification_ref":"TestOutputFilesExist::test_all_output_files_exist","verification":"A grader can verify this by iterating over every task entry in problem.json and confirming os.path.exists is true for the file at that task's plan_output path.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S-01","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S-02","requirement":"Each plan file must contain exactly one action primitive per line, with no empty lines and no line containing more than one action.","source_quote":"Each action primitive should be written on a line.","verification_type":"assertion","verification_ref":"TestNumericalCorrectness::test_allclose","verification":"A grader can verify this by reading each plan file line by line and confirming no line is empty and that every line contains exactly one \"(\" and one \")\", as performed by check_plan_format.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S-02","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S-03","requirement":"Each line of the plan must be a syntactically correct PDDL action in the form action_name(obj1, obj2, ...) that can be parsed without error.","source_quote":"The plan should be a syntactically correct PDDL plan.","verification_type":"assertion","verification_ref":"TestNumericalCorrectness::test_allclose","verification":"A grader can verify this by matching each plan line against a name(args) pattern and confirming parsing does not raise a malformed-line error.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S-03","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S-04","requirement":"Every action name and every object name used in the plan must correspond to an actual action or object defined in the task's PDDL domain and problem files.","source_quote":"Action names and object names in the generated plan should match the PDDL domain and PDDL problem.","verification_type":"assertion","verification_ref":"TestNumericalCorrectness::test_allclose","verification":"A grader can verify this by resolving each action name and each argument name in the plan against the parsed domain's action schemas and the problem's object list, confirming none are unrecognized.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S-04","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S-05","requirement":"The plan must be a valid solution: executing it from the problem's initial state according to the domain's action semantics must actually reach a goal state.","source_quote":"The plan should be valid, it should solve the problem when executed according to the PDDL grammar.","verification_type":"assertion","verification_ref":"TestNumericalCorrectness::test_allclose","verification":"A grader can verify this by running a sequential plan validator against the parsed domain, problem, and the agent's plan, and confirming the validation status is VALID.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S-05","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S-06","requirement":"Every task entry listed in problem.json must be addressed using its own specified domain and problem files, producing a corresponding valid plan output — no task entries may be skipped.","source_quote":"For each task specified in `problem.json`, you need to: First, load the PDDL domain file and PDDL problem file. Second, generate a PDDL plan for solving the planning problem.","verification_type":"assertion","verification_ref":"TestOutputFilesExist::test_all_output_files_exist; TestNumericalCorrectness::test_allclose","verification":"A grader can verify this by iterating over all tasks in problem.json and confirming each one has a plan_output file that exists and passes plan validation against its own listed domain and problem files.","notes":"Loading of the domain/problem files themselves is not directly observable as an artifact; it is verified indirectly through successful parsing and validation of each task's plan.","validation":{"item_id":"S-06","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}}],"runs":[{"id":"claude-code-no-skills__aws-bedrock-anthropic-claude-opus-4-7||pddl-tpp-planning","label":"claude-code-no-skills · aws-bedrock-ant